.TH FS 4
.SH NAME
fs \- the file server (port "fs")
.SH SYNOPSIS
Port
.BR fs .
A boot module, started by
.BR init (8);
restarted if it ends.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fs
offers SieFS
.RB ( siefs (5))
on the disk served by
.BR vblk (4)
to every program.
The SieFS library does the file system; the server adds what a shared
service needs:
.TP
.B paths
absolute ("/a/b"), walked component by component, following symbolic links,
checking at each step that the caller may go there;
.TP
.B permissions
from the uid and gid the kernel stamps on every message (and the caller's
other groups, asked only when they matter), so no program can lie about who
it is; root, uid 0, may do anything;
.TP
.B open files
a table of handles, each valid only for the process that opened it;
.TP
.B commits
changes reach the disk 1 s after the first one, or at
.BR FS_SYNC ,
all together: a crash loses at most that last second and never leaves a
half-done change;
.TP
.B volumes and snapshots
a user's home is a volume of its own, encrypted; a helper thread takes a
snapshot of every volume each hour ("auto-YYYY-MM-DD-HH00"), kept a day.
When the disk is full, a write first lets the oldest hourly snapshot go.
.PP
Programs use it through the library (user/lib/fs.c:
.BR fs_open ,
.BR fs_read ,
\&...), which also reopens their files when the server was restarted.
.SH MESSAGES
Paths travel in sbuf; two paths ("from\e0to") or a path and a name are
separated by a 0. A read or write moves at most FS_MAX (128 KiB).
.TP
.B FS_OPEN
path; w[1] = flags (FS_RDONLY, FS_WRONLY, FS_RDWR, FS_CREAT, FS_EXCL,
FS_TRUNC, FS_APPEND, FS_DIRECTORY), w[2] = the mode of a new file \-> handle.
.TP
.BR FS_CLOSE ", " FS_FSTAT ", " FS_TRUNCATE
w[1] = handle (FS_TRUNCATE: w[2] = size; FS_FSTAT: a siefs_stat_t in rbuf).
.TP
.BR FS_READ ", " FS_WRITE
w[1] = handle, w[2] = offset (ignored with FS_APPEND), w[3] = size; data in
rbuf or sbuf \-> bytes.
.TP
.B FS_READDIR
w[1] = handle, w[2] = cursor; rbuf <\- records {u64 object, u8 type, u8 name
length, name} \-> bytes, reply w[1] = the next cursor.
.TP
.BR FS_STAT ", " FS_READLINK ", " FS_MKDIR ", " FS_UNLINK ", " FS_RMDIR ", " FS_CHMOD ", " FS_CHOWN ", " FS_UTIMES
path, and w[1], w[2] as the operation needs (a mode; uid and gid, root only;
times in ns).
.TP
.BR FS_RENAME ", " FS_LINK ", " FS_SYMLINK
"from\e0to" ("existing\e0new", "target\e0path").
.TP
.BR FS_GETXATTR ", " FS_SETXATTR ", " FS_LISTXATTR ", " FS_RMXATTR
attributes (tags): "path\e0name[\e0value]".
.TP
.B FS_FIND
"name\e0value", w[1] = cursor; rbuf <\- the paths of the files with that
attribute \-> bytes, reply w[1] = the next cursor (0: done).
.TP
.BR FS_STATFS ", " FS_SYNC
the volume's size and free space (siefs_statfs_t); commit now.
.TP
.B FS_SNAP
"path\e0name", w[1] = SNAP_TAKE, SNAP_DELETE, SNAP_RESTORE or SNAP_LIST (w[2] =
cursor): root, or the owner of the volume's root.
.TP
.BR FS_MKVOL ", " FS_RMVOL
(root) a new volume mounted at path, with w[1] = mode, w[2] = uid, w[3] =
gid, encrypted when an fs_key_t follows the path; remove an empty one.
.TP
.BR FS_UNLOCK ", " FS_REKEY ", " FS_KDF
(root:
.BR auth (8))
open an encrypted volume with its key-encryption key; wrap its key anew; how
that key is derived. The file server never sees a password.
.SH ERRORS
\-EACCES, \-EPERM (not allowed), \-ENOENT, \-EEXIST, \-ENOTDIR, \-EISDIR,
\-ENOTEMPTY, \-ENOSPC, \-EROFS (a snapshot), \-EXDEV (across volumes),
\-EBUSY (a mounted volume), \-EBADF (not your handle).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR siefs (5),
.BR vblk (4),
.BR snap (1),
.BR tag (1),
.BR security (7)
